<%# Layout for the square marketplace listing slides (hero, what's included,
    how it works, about).

    Separate from layouts/pdf_printable.html.erb on purpose. That layout is the
    printed product and is measured in inches and points; this one is a 1280px
    marketing canvas that is never printed. They shared a layout once — the
    gallery was the print sheet scaled to 0.73 on a square mat — and the result
    was honest but unsellable next to purpose-built competitor art.

    Ported from speakanyway-printables:
      src/templates/shared/branded-frame.css   -> the shared chrome below
      src/templates/previews/hero-board.css    -> .hero-*
      src/templates/previews/whats-included.css-> .included-*
      src/plugins/aac/templates/previews/about-saw.css -> .steps-strip, .founder-band

    Rails is authoritative for listings created from the Rails admin; that repo
    is authoritative for the ones its pipeline originates. They may differ —
    just not by accident. Same rule as Etsy::CopyRules.

    Three rails hold across every change here:

    1. Nothing is fetched over the network by the CHROME. Nunito is inlined by
       Boards::Printables::Fonts, and the logo, founder photo and room scenes by
       Boards::Printables::BrandAssets. The one documented exception is the
       board symbol art inside a page thumbnail, which loads from the CDN
       exactly as it does when the product PDF is printed.
    2. No emoji, and no decorative glyph outside Nunito's coverage. The render
       box's Chrome has no guaranteed colour-emoji font, so the pipeline's
       emoji step icons and its "*", "•" and arrow pseudo-element bullets would
       ship as tofu boxes to Etsy. Icons are inline SVG; bullets are CSS-drawn
       shapes. This repo already learned this once with U+2713 on the printed
       what's-included page.
    3. Every band is a fixed height and the copy that fills it is bounded
       (Printables::SlideCopy). Text that outgrows its band doesn't wrap here,
       it overlaps the next one.
    4. Colour that varies per listing comes from ONE place: the four tokens
       Boards::Printables::Palette writes over the defaults below. Hardcoding a
       brand hex in a rule takes it out of the rotation and every listing in the
       shop starts sharing it again. The fixed colours — navy title banner,
       white paper cards, black ribbon, navy-on-white QR — are the ones carrying
       contrast, and they stay out of the palette on purpose.
    5. No CONTENT sits within `--safe-x` of the left or right edge. Etsy does
       not letterbox a square photo — the listing page frames it 4:5 and
       cover-crops, taking 10% off each side, and the seller-side "Adjust
       thumbnail" dialog crops again for the search grid. At the old 56px inset
       that removed the first letter of the board name and better than half the
       QR, on every listing in the shop. Backgrounds and band fills still bleed
       edge to edge; it is only the type, the QR and the logo that move in. A
       new inset written as a literal px value reintroduces the bug. %>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <title>Listing image</title>

    <style>
      <%= Boards::Printables::Fonts.face_css %>

      <%# The fixed half of the palette: the colours that carry contrast on
          every slide and therefore never rotate. Every OTHER brand colour lives
          in Boards::Printables::Palette — there is deliberately nothing to
          hardcode a hex from here. %>
      :root {
        --navy: #13496f;
        --cream: #FBF7F1;
        --white: #FFFFFF;
        --ink: #1A1A1A;

        --shadow-soft: 0 18px 40px rgba(19, 73, 111, 0.18);
        --shadow-card: 0 24px 56px rgba(19, 73, 111, 0.28);

        /* The canvas edge length. Gallery slides render at 1280; the listing
           video's frames override this to 1080 (.video-frame below). */
        --slide-px: 1280px;

        /* The horizontal safe zone — see rail 5 at the top of this file.
           12.5% of the 1280px canvas, against the 10% Etsy actually takes off
           each side, and a larger fraction still at the video's 1080. Every
           inset that holds CONTENT reads this token; band backgrounds still
           bleed to the edge. */
        --safe-x: 160px;

        /* Rotated per listing by Boards::Printables::Palette. These defaults
           are its first entry, so a slide rendered without a palette (a spec,
           a preview) still looks like a real one instead of an unstyled one. */
        --accent: #5b2a86;
        --accent-soft: #c9f08a;
        --band: #c9f08a;
        --surface: linear-gradient(180deg, #b9e2ff 0%, #dff0ff 60%, #f7fbff 100%);
      }

      <%# Emitted after the defaults so it wins on cascade order — no rule below
          needs to know a palette exists, they all read the tokens. %>
      <%= @palette_css %>

      * {
        box-sizing: border-box;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
      }

      html, body {
        -webkit-print-color-adjust: exact;
        print-color-adjust: exact;
      }

      body {
        font-family: Nunito, system-ui, -apple-system, Segoe UI, Roboto, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
        color: var(--ink);
        background: var(--cream);
        -webkit-font-smoothing: antialiased;
      }

      p, li { text-wrap: pretty; hyphens: none; }

      /* ── The canvas ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
      /* Square, because that is the shape Etsy's search grid uses and the one
         all six slides share. It is NOT the shape every view uses — see rail 5
         above; the safe zone is what makes a square source survive the crops. */
      /* Square, and sized from a token because two renderers share this layout
         at different edge lengths: RenderListingImages::CANVAS_PX (1280) for
         the gallery slides and RenderListingVideo::FRAME_PX (1080) for the
         video frames. A hardcoded 1280 here rendered a video frame clipped to
         its viewport rather than fitted to it. */
      .slide {
        position: relative;
        width: var(--slide-px);
        height: var(--slide-px);
        overflow: hidden;
        background: var(--cream);
      }

      /* Background photo as an <img>, not a CSS url() — the src is a data URI
         and this keeps the encoding in one place (BrandAssets). */
      .slide .bg-image {
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        object-fit: cover;
        z-index: 0;
      }

      /* The room photos are bright and busy; without this the navy title and
         the white paper cards both lose contrast against a sunlit window. The
         top stop is the heaviest because that is where the hero's largest type
         sits. */
      .slide .bg-scrim {
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        z-index: 1;
        background: linear-gradient(
          180deg,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0.93) 0%,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0.45) 38%,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0.62) 72%,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0.90) 100%
        );
      }

      .slide.bg-sky {
        background:
          radial-gradient(circle at 20% 18%, rgba(255,255,255,0.9) 0 110px, transparent 140px),
          radial-gradient(circle at 78% 22%, rgba(255,255,255,0.75) 0 70px, transparent 110px),
          var(--surface);
      }

      /* ── Slide stack ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
      /* Everything except the hero lays out as a flex column rather than a set
         of absolutely-positioned bands at hand-tuned offsets. The bands drift:
         a board with a long name grows its card, an overflow caption appears
         only for a set, and the fixed offsets that looked right for one
         printable had the caption colliding with a tile label on the next.
         A column can't collide with itself. */
      .slide-stack {
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        z-index: 4;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: flex-start;
        gap: 20px;
        padding: 52px var(--safe-x) 56px;
      }

      /* The footer strip is anchored to the bottom edge, so the column has to
         stop above it. Keep this in step with .footer-strip's min-height. */
      .slide-stack.has-footer { padding-bottom: 250px; }

      .slide-stack > * { flex: 0 0 auto; max-width: 100%; }

      /* The one element that absorbs the leftover height, so a short board and
         a tall one both produce a balanced slide. */
      .slide-stack > .stretch {
        flex: 1 1 auto;
        min-height: 0;
        width: 100%;
      }

      /* ── Shared chrome ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
      .logo-corner {
        position: absolute;
        z-index: 5;
        top: 34px;
        right: var(--safe-x);
        width: 108px;
        height: 108px;
      }

      /* Sized for an Etsy search grid, where the whole slide is ~230px wide.
         At 52px the board name was a smudge at thumbnail size; 64px is the
         smallest that still reads there. */
      .title-banner {
        position: relative;
        z-index: 4;
        display: inline-block;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 22px 38px;
        background: var(--navy);
        color: var(--white);
        border-radius: 18px;
        font-weight: 800;
        font-size: 64px;
        line-height: 1.05;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
      }

      .title-banner.small { font-size: 54px; }

      .headline-callout {
        position: relative;
        z-index: 4;
        display: inline-block;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 16px 30px;
        background: var(--white);
        color: var(--ink);
        border-radius: 14px;
        font-weight: 700;
        font-size: 38px;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
      }

      /* The claim that separates this from every other AAC printable on the
         marketplace, so it gets its own object rather than a bullet in the
         footer. Accent-coloured, so it rotates with the listing. */
      .audio-badge {
        position: relative;
        z-index: 4;
        display: inline-flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 14px;
        padding: 16px 28px;
        background: var(--accent);
        color: var(--white);
        border-radius: 999px;
        font-weight: 800;
        font-size: 27px;
        letter-spacing: 0.04em;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
      }

      /* Inline SVG, never an emoji — see rail 2 at the top of this file. */
      .audio-badge svg {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        width: 38px;
        height: 38px;
        fill: none;
        stroke: currentColor;
        stroke-width: 4;
        stroke-linecap: round;
        stroke-linejoin: round;
      }

      /* The one claim that answers "do I have to wait for this in the post?" */
      .instant-ribbon {
        position: absolute;
        z-index: 6;
        top: 0;
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
        padding: 16px var(--safe-x);
        background: var(--ink);
        color: var(--white);
        font-weight: 800;
        font-size: 25px;
        letter-spacing: 0.1em;
        text-align: center;
      }

      .slide.has-ribbon .title-banner { margin-top: 96px; }

      .accent-panel {
        position: absolute;
        z-index: 4;
        border-radius: 18px;
        padding: 26px 30px;
        color: var(--white);
        background: var(--accent);
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
        font-weight: 700;
        font-size: 27px;
        line-height: 1.25;
      }

      .accent-panel .accent-eyebrow {
        display: block;
        font-size: 17px;
        letter-spacing: 0.1em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        opacity: 0.85;
        margin-bottom: 6px;
      }

      .accent-panel .accent-sub {
        display: block;
        margin-top: 10px;
        font-weight: 600;
        font-size: 21px;
        opacity: 0.95;
      }

      /* ── CSS-drawn bullets (never a glyph) ────────────────────────────── */
      .bullets { list-style: none; }

      .bullets li {
        position: relative;
        padding-left: 30px;
      }

      .bullets li::before {
        content: "";
        position: absolute;
        left: 0;
        top: 0.55em;
        width: 14px;
        height: 14px;
        border-radius: 4px;
        transform: rotate(45deg);
        background: var(--accent);
      }

      .bullets.on-dark li::before { background: var(--accent-soft); }

      /* ── Footer strip ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
      /* Every slide that has one lays it out the same way: copy on the left,
         QR on the right, site mark along the bottom. A buyer flicking through
         five gallery images shouldn't have to re-find the QR on each one. */
      .footer-strip {
        position: absolute;
        z-index: 4;
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
        bottom: 0;
        min-height: 226px;
        padding: 24px var(--safe-x) 46px;
        background: var(--band);
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 34px;
      }

      .footer-strip .footer-bullets { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }

      /* Small on purpose: it is a signature, not a call to action, and the QR
         beside it is what a buyer is meant to act on. */
      .footer-strip .site-mark {
        position: absolute;
        left: var(--safe-x);
        bottom: 16px;
        font-size: 19px;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        color: var(--navy);
        opacity: 0.72;
      }

      .footer-strip .footer-title {
        font-weight: 800;
        font-size: 25px;
        letter-spacing: 0.03em;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        margin-bottom: 10px;
        color: var(--navy);
      }

      .footer-strip ul { font-size: 21px; line-height: 1.5; }

      .footer-strip .footer-qr {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 8px;
      }

      .footer-strip .footer-qr img {
        width: 148px;
        height: 148px;
        padding: 8px;
        background: var(--white);
        border-radius: 12px;
      }

      /* Capped so a longer caption wraps under the code instead of widening the
         column and squeezing the bullets beside it. */
      .footer-strip .footer-qr .qr-caption {
        max-width: 190px;
        font-size: 19px;
        line-height: 1.25;
        font-weight: 800;
        color: var(--navy);
        text-align: center;
      }

      /* ── Paper card: how a board page is presented on every slide ─────── */
      /* The thumbnail arrives already trimmed to its content
         (RenderPageThumbnails), so the card hugs the page rather than forcing
         it to a fixed box. A white sheet with a shadow is what makes it read as
         a printed page.

         The card is sized by an explicit aspect-ratio, written inline from the
         thumbnail's real pixel dimensions. It used to be `height: auto` on the
         image plus `max-height: 100%`, and that percentage resolves against a
         card whose own height is indefinite — which per CSS means it is ignored
         entirely. The image rendered full height, the card was capped, and
         `overflow: hidden` silently sliced the bottom off every page. A ratio
         plus two max constraints is definite, so the box fits its slot and
         nothing is cut. Do not reintroduce a percentage height here. */
      .paper {
        background: var(--white);
        border-radius: 10px;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
        overflow: hidden;
        align-self: center;
        max-width: 100%;
        max-height: 100%;
      }

      .paper img {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        object-fit: contain;
        background: var(--white);
      }

      /* ── Hero ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
      /* The ribbon overlays the top edge, so the hero's column starts below it. */
      .slide-stack.has-ribbon { padding-top: 108px; }

      .hero-stage {
        position: relative;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: 22px;
      }

      .hero-stage .paper { flex: 1 1 0; }

      /* A single page gets the whole stage — which the safe zone has already
         narrowed to 960px, so this needs no cap of its own. */
      .hero-stage.single .paper { max-width: 100%; }

      /* A set fans into an overlapping pile rather than a tidy row. Three
         side-by-side cards on the 960px stage are ~300px wide, and a board page
         at that size is a coloured smudge — the buyer can count the pages but
         can't see a single word. Overlapping buys each card ~50% more width
         and reads as "more than one board" just as fast. */
      /* The widths have to close: n cards at W overlapping by V occupy
         nW - (n-1)V, and anything over 100% runs the outer pages off the slide
         edge. Boards::Printables::HeroFan holds the per-n table and asserts
         that sum stays under 98%, which leaves room for the rotation to bleed
         without clipping.

         --fan-w, --fan-overlap, --rot and --z are written inline by hero.html.erb
         rather than being an nth-child ladder here. The ladder only covered
         three cards, so raising HERO_TILES left cards four and five with no
         transform, no negative margin and no z-index — 200% of the stage, half
         of it off the slide. A rule that has to be edited whenever a constant
         moves is a rule that will be forgotten. */
      .hero-stage.fan { gap: 0; }

      /* Makes room for the sticker. Slightly narrower cards beat a sticker
         sitting on top of one. */
      .hero-stage.has-sticker { padding-left: 96px; }

      .hero-stage.fan .paper {
        flex: 0 0 var(--fan-w);
        transform: rotate(var(--rot));
        z-index: var(--z);
      }

      .hero-stage.fan .paper + .paper { margin-left: calc(-1 * var(--fan-overlap)); }

      /* The bundle count, over the top-left of the pile. A sticker rather than
         another line in the column because the hero is judged at ~230px wide in
         an Etsy search grid: at that size the headline that says the same thing
         is an illegible smudge and a single numeral is still a numeral.
         z-index sits above every card in the fan (HeroFan hands out at most
         MAX_CARDS). */
      /* The sticker must clip the CORNER of the pile, not cover a page. The fan
         runs to 98% of the stage, so there is no free space to sit in — the
         stage is indented instead (.hero-stage.has-sticker below) and the
         sticker fills the gap that opens up. The outermost card is the least
         important one (RenderListingImages puts the root in the middle), but a
         listing photo that hides a page it is advertising still reads as a
         mistake.

         `left: 0`, never a negative value: the sticker carries the biggest
         numeral on the slide and .slide-stack's --safe-x inset is the only
         thing keeping it out of Etsy's 4:5 side crop. See rail 5. A negative
         top is fine — Etsy crops the sides, not the top. */
      .count-sticker {
        position: absolute;
        z-index: 20;
        top: -20px;
        left: 0;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        width: 176px;
        height: 176px;
        padding: 10px;
        border: 8px solid var(--white);
        border-radius: 50%;
        background: var(--accent);
        color: var(--white);
        text-align: center;
        transform: rotate(-8deg);
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
      }

      .count-sticker strong {
        font-size: 82px;
        font-weight: 800;
        line-height: 0.9;
      }

      .count-sticker span {
        /* The label carries its own line break, so the two words stack without
           depending on the width happening to wrap them. */
        white-space: pre-line;
        font-size: 22px;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 0.06em;
        line-height: 1.1;
      }

      /* Names the three print versions instead of multiplying them into the
         count — see SlideCopy.hero_count_badge for why "18 PAGES" is the wrong
         number to print here. */
      .variant-strip {
        position: relative;
        z-index: 4;
        align-self: stretch;
        padding: 14px 24px;
        border-radius: 12px;
        background: var(--band);
        color: var(--navy);
        font-weight: 800;
        font-size: 26px;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
        text-align: center;
      }

      /* ── Listing video frames ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
      /* Rendered at 1080 square rather than the slides' 1280, so everything
         here is sized against that rather than reusing the slide bands. They
         share the layout for the tokens, the inlined fonts and the palette —
         the clip and the still gallery have to read as one listing.

         The safe zone still applies, and harder: Etsy crops a listing video to
         a 4:5 frame the same way it crops a photo, and there is no
         "adjust thumbnail" dialog to rescue a cropped word. */
      .video-frame {
        --slide-px: 1080px;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        background: var(--surface);
      }

      .video-stack {
        position: relative;
        z-index: 4;
        display: flex;
        flex: 1 1 auto;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: 28px;
        padding: 96px var(--safe-x);
        text-align: center;
      }

      .video-headline {
        font-size: 58px;
        font-weight: 800;
        line-height: 1.15;
        color: var(--navy);
        background: rgba(255,255,255,0.94);
        border-radius: 20px;
        padding: 26px 34px;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
      }

      .video-sub {
        font-size: 32px;
        font-weight: 700;
        color: var(--ink);
      }

      .video-count {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        width: 236px;
        height: 236px;
        border: 10px solid var(--white);
        border-radius: 50%;
        background: var(--accent);
        color: var(--white);
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
      }

      .video-count strong { font-size: 116px; font-weight: 800; line-height: 0.9; }

      .video-count span {
        white-space: pre-line;
        font-size: 28px;
        font-weight: 800;
        letter-spacing: 0.06em;
        line-height: 1.1;
      }

      /* A page frame gives the sheet nearly the whole 1080, because the page IS
         the content — the head and foot only exist to say it belongs to a set. */
      .video-page { padding: 34px var(--safe-x); gap: 22px; }

      .video-page-head {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        gap: 20px;
      }

      .video-page-label {
        flex: 1 1 auto;
        overflow: hidden;
        white-space: nowrap;
        text-overflow: ellipsis;
        padding: 14px 26px;
        border-radius: 14px;
        background: var(--navy);
        color: var(--white);
        font-size: 40px;
        font-weight: 800;
        text-align: left;
      }

      .video-page-count {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        padding: 14px 26px;
        border-radius: 999px;
        background: var(--accent);
        color: var(--white);
        font-size: 36px;
        font-weight: 800;
      }

      /* The page fits itself into whatever height is left, at its own aspect.
         Deliberately NOT an aspect-ratio on a flex-grow item: `flex: 1 1 auto`
         resolves the height first and aspect-ratio then has nothing to say, so
         a landscape page rendered as a tall white box with the board floating
         in the middle of it. A centring wrapper plus max-width/max-height on
         the image itself can't get this wrong, and a rendered page already has
         its own white paper — it never needed a card behind it. */
      .video-paper {
        flex: 1 1 auto;
        min-height: 0;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
      }

      .video-paper img {
        max-width: 100%;
        max-height: 100%;
        border-radius: 16px;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
      }

      .video-page-foot { flex: 0 0 auto; }

      .video-back {
        padding: 16px 28px;
        border-radius: 999px;
        background: var(--band);
        color: var(--navy);
        font-size: 34px;
        font-weight: 800;
        text-align: center;
      }

      /* The first page has nothing to go back TO, so it carries the download
         claim in the same slot rather than leaving a hole the eye tracks. */
      .video-back.is-root {
        background: var(--ink);
        color: var(--white);
        font-size: 28px;
        letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      }

      .video-outro-row {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: 40px;
        width: 100%;
      }

      .video-qr {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        padding: 18px;
        border-radius: 20px;
        background: var(--white);
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
      }

      .video-qr img { display: block; width: 244px; height: 244px; }

      .video-device { flex: 0 1 auto; min-width: 0; }

      .video-device img {
        display: block;
        max-width: 100%;
        max-height: 300px;
        border-radius: 14px;
      }

      .video-outro .site-mark { font-size: 28px; }

      .hero-empty {
        font-size: 30px;
        font-weight: 800;
        color: var(--navy);
        background: rgba(255,255,255,0.9);
        border-radius: 18px;
        padding: 40px 48px;
        text-align: center;
      }

      /* ── What's included ──────────────────────────────────────────────── */
      /* Rows are explicit, not auto: the page cards size themselves against
         their slot, and a slot with an indefinite height gives them nothing to
         measure against. Capped in height too — the grid is there to show what
         a buyer gets, not to be the slide, and the pages were big enough to
         crowd out the panel that lists what's in the download. */
      /* A white card holding the grid and its overflow caption, so the tiles
         read as a contents page rather than as thumbnails scattered on the
         background.

         margin: auto 0 is load-bearing. The stage is a flex item that grows,
         but the max-height stops it short — and the leftover space then piles
         up below it as a dead band. Auto margins in a flex column absorb that
         space evenly, so the stage centres and the panel below sits on the
         bottom edge where it belongs. */
      .included-stage {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 14px;
        max-height: 700px;
        margin: auto 0;
        padding: 30px 30px 24px;
        background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
        border-radius: 24px;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
      }

      .tile-grid {
        flex: 1 1 auto;
        min-height: 0;
        display: grid;
        gap: 18px;
        align-content: center;
        align-items: center;
        justify-items: center;
      }

      .tile {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 10px;
        width: 100%;
        min-width: 0;
        min-height: 0;
        max-height: 100%;
      }

      /* Every card is the SAME shape, unlike the hero's, where each page keeps
         its own. Boards differ wildly in proportion — a 12x3 grid next to a
         6x8 one — and cards cut to each board's own aspect made a ragged row
         that read as a layout bug rather than as a contents page. The page is
         letterboxed inside the card with object-fit: contain, and since the
         card is white and so is the page, the letterboxing reads as margin.
         Nothing is clipped: the box is definite and the image is contained. */
      /* Capped by WIDTH, never by a percentage height: the card's height falls
         out of its width and the ratio, so a width cap is what keeps a row of
         one or two boards from growing taller than its slot. A percentage
         height here would be measured against an indefinite parent and ignored,
         which is the trap that clipped these cards in the first place. */
      .tile .paper {
        width: 100%;
        max-width: var(--tile-max, 300px);
        aspect-ratio: 3 / 2;
      }

      /* Plain text, not a pill: eight navy pills on a white card read as eight
         buttons. The card behind the grid is already doing the containing. */
      .tile .tile-label {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        max-width: 100%;
        color: var(--navy);
        font-size: 23px;
        font-weight: 800;
        text-align: center;
        white-space: nowrap;
        overflow: hidden;
        text-overflow: ellipsis;
      }

      .overflow-note {
        width: 100%;
        text-align: center;
        font-size: 25px;
        font-weight: 800;
        color: var(--navy);
      }

      .included-panel {
        width: 100%;
        background: var(--ink);
        color: var(--white);
        border-radius: 18px;
        padding: 24px 30px;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
      }

      .included-panel .included-title {
        font-weight: 800;
        font-size: 27px;
        margin-bottom: 12px;
      }

      .included-panel ul { font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.5; }

      /* ── How it works ─────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
      /* Capped, then centred by the auto margins — the same trick as .tile-grid
         and for the same reason. Left to stretch the full slide, the four cards
         are 700px tall with two lines of text marooned at the top of each; left
         to hug their copy, they float in the middle of a large empty band. */
      .steps-strip {
        width: 100%;
        display: flex;
        align-items: stretch;
        gap: 20px;
        max-height: 470px;
        margin: auto 0;
      }

      .step-card {
        position: relative;
        flex: 1;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        justify-content: center;
        background: var(--white);
        border-radius: 22px;
        padding: 64px 24px 44px;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
        text-align: center;
      }

      .step-card .step-num {
        position: absolute;
        top: -24px;
        left: 50%;
        transform: translateX(-50%);
        width: 64px;
        height: 64px;
        border-radius: 50%;
        background: var(--accent);
        color: var(--white);
        font-weight: 800;
        font-size: 32px;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
      }

      /* Inline SVG, never an emoji — see rail 2 at the top of this file. */
      .step-card .step-icon {
        width: 84px;
        height: 84px;
        margin: 0 auto 20px;
        display: block;
        fill: none;
        stroke: var(--accent);
        stroke-width: 5;
        stroke-linecap: round;
        stroke-linejoin: round;
      }

      .step-card .step-title {
        font-weight: 800;
        font-size: 32px;
        margin-bottom: 12px;
        color: var(--navy);
      }

      .step-card .step-body {
        font-size: 22px;
        line-height: 1.4;
        color: #333;
      }

      /* ── On a device ──────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
      /* The stage lays out in the scene photo's own pixel space and is scaled
         and offset to cover the slide as one unit, so the screen quad in
         TabletScene stays in lockstep with where the photo actually is. */
      .mockup-stage {
        position: absolute;
        top: 0;
        left: 0;
        z-index: 0;
        transform-origin: 0 0;
      }

      .mockup-photo {
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        display: block;
      }

      /* Warped onto the tablet's glass by the inline matrix3d. transform-origin
         must be 0 0 — the homography maps the element's own top-left corner to
         the quad's, and a centred origin silently offsets the whole solve. */
      .mockup-screen {
        position: absolute;
        top: 0;
        left: 0;
        transform-origin: 0 0;
        background: var(--white);
        overflow: hidden;
      }

      /* contain, so a board of any shape sits on the screen letterboxed rather
         than stretched to fill it — a squashed board reads as a distorted
         product, which is the opposite of what this slide is for. */
      .mockup-screen img {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        object-fit: contain;
        background: var(--white);
      }

      /* Faint diagonal glare, so the board reads as a lit display rather than
         as a picture pasted onto the glass. */
      .mockup-screen::after {
        content: "";
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        background: linear-gradient(
          115deg,
          rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.20) 0%,
          rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06) 38%,
          rgba(255, 255, 255, 0) 62%
        );
      }

      /* These room photos are dim and warm; the scrim is what keeps the navy
         title and the footer readable over them. Top and bottom only, so the
         tablet in the middle stays untouched.

         The top half is deliberately light and fades out early: the title
         banner and the audio badge carry their own solid fills, so they don't
         need the scrim to be legible — and the tablet's own screen starts at
         ~23% of the slide, where a heavier gradient washes out the app chrome
         and makes the screenshot read as dim rather than lit. */
      .mockup-scrim {
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        z-index: 1;
        background: linear-gradient(
          180deg,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0.90) 0%,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0.35) 15%,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0) 27%,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0) 68%,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0.70) 88%,
          rgba(251, 247, 241, 0.90) 100%
        );
      }

      /* ── About ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
      /* Hugs its copy and floats in the middle of what's left, rather than
         stretching: a stretched band is a 700px-tall white card with three
         lines of text marooned in the centre of it. */
      .founder-band {
        width: 100%;
        margin: auto 0;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 48px;
        padding: 60px 52px;
        background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
        border-radius: 24px;
        box-shadow: var(--shadow-card);
      }

      /* A frame with the photo cropped inside it, so the row can't reflow if
         the photo is ever swapped for one with a different aspect, and so the
         image can't spill past the circle.

         The source (brittany-founder.jpg) is a framed head-and-shoulders
         portrait at 484x522. Cropping to a square leaves ~8% vertical overflow,
         which is what object-position moves within — enough to sit the eyes
         above centre, which is where a portrait wants them.

         Worth knowing if the photo is replaced: object-position only does
         anything when there is overflow to pan. The previous source was square,
         so in a square frame it moved nothing at all and the face sat off to
         one side of the circle no matter what was declared here. Check the
         source's aspect before trusting this line.

         Sized at 270px against a 484px-wide source: at the slides' device scale
         of 2 that is 540 device pixels, so the photo is barely upscaled. */
      .founder-photo {
        flex: 0 0 auto;
        width: 270px;
        height: 270px;
        border-radius: 50%;
        overflow: hidden;
        border: 6px solid var(--white);
        box-shadow: 0 6px 18px rgba(19, 73, 111, 0.22);
      }

      .founder-photo img {
        display: block;
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        object-fit: cover;
        object-position: 50% 42%;
      }

      .founder-copy { flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; }

      .founder-hi {
        font-size: 44px;
        font-weight: 800;
        margin-bottom: 16px;
        color: var(--accent);
      }

      .founder-copy p {
        font-size: 27px;
        line-height: 1.45;
        margin-bottom: 14px;
      }

      .founder-copy p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; font-weight: 700; }
    </style>
  </head>

  <body>
    <%= yield %>
  </body>
</html>
